1. AWS Backup: cross-account copy
Example: Your production EC2 instances are in Account A, and your company wants backups protected in a separate Account B.
Implementation steps
1. In the AWS Organizations management account, enable AWS Backup cross-account backup.
2. In Account B, create a destination backup vault and configure its resource-based access policy to allow the approved source account to copy backups into it.
3. Configure the required IAM permissions in Account A for initiating and copying backups.
4. Configure KMS permissions and encryption keys appropriately. Customer-managed KMS keys may need policies allowing the required cross-account use.
5. Create an AWS Backup plan in Account A. Set the backup schedule and retention, then add a copy rule pointing to Account B's destination vault.
6. Monitor backup and copy job statuses, and test restoration from Account B.
Cross-account backup generally requires the accounts to belong to the same AWS Organization, and support varies by resource type and Region.


2. S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)
Suppose your source bucket is in us-east-1 and your destination bucket is in ap-south-1 (Mumbai).

Source S3 bucket
Account A · us-east-1
      |
S3 Replication Rule (CRR)
IAM role · bucket permissions · encryption
      |
Destination S3 bucket
Account B · ap-south-1

When a new object is uploaded to the source bucket, S3 can automatically replicate it to the destination bucket based on your replication rule. Both buckets must have versioning enabled. 

AWS re:Post



How to implement CRR
1. Create the source and destination S3 buckets in their respective Regions.
2. Enable S3 Versioning on both buckets.
3. In the source bucket, open Management → Replication rules → Create replication rule.
4. Specify whether to replicate all objects or only selected prefixes/tags.
5. Configure the destination bucket. If it's in another account, provide the destination account details and authorize the replication role in the destination bucket policy.
6. Configure the required IAM permissions and KMS permissions if using SSE-KMS encryption.
7. Save the rule and upload a test object. Verify the object's replication status.
By default, a newly created replication rule applies to eligible new objects; use S3 Batch Replication if you also need to replicate existing objects. 

